  "name": "Question 10",
  "problem": "Negate the following statement:\n\n$\\,$ Every real $2\\times 2$ matrix is not singular.",
  "worked_solution": "Let ${M}_{2}(R)$ be the set of all real $2\\times 2$ matrices. The given statement can be re-phrased as follows:\n\n$\\,$ For all $x\\in {M}_{2}(R),$ $x$ is not singular.\n\nIntroducing the universal quantifier, we get the following:\n\n$\\,$ $∀x\\in {M}_{2}(R),\\,x\\text{ is not singular}.$\n\nNegating this, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(∀x\\in {M}_{2}(R),\\,x\\text{ is not singular}) & ≡∃x\\in {M}_{2}(R),\\,¬(x\\text{ is not singular}) \\\\  & ≡∃x\\in {M}_{2}(R),\\,x\\text{ is singular}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich reads \n\n$\\,$ There exists $x\\in {M}_{2}(R)$ such that $x$ is singular.\n\nor simply\n\n$\\,$ Some real $2\\times 2$ matrices are singular.",
